# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts.
#
# These are anchored to the repo root, and that is not fussiness. The
# unanchored `coverage.*` that used to be here matched cmd/domain/coverage.go —
# the implementation of `domain expansion: coverage` — so `git add` skipped it
# in silence, the file lived on in every working tree that created it, and the
# break only surfaced when someone built from a source tarball: `undefined:
# parseCoverageArgs`. A pattern for build output should never be able to
# swallow a source file, so it names the files the tools actually write.
/coverage.out
/coverage.html
/coverage.txt
/profile.cov
*.coverprofile
*.out

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# Nix build outputs
result
result-*

# VS Code extension build artifacts
editors/vscode/node_modules/
editors/vscode/*.vsix
editors/vscode/package-lock.json

# the compiler itself, built at the repo root per docs/wasm/build.sh
# ("go build -o domain ./cmd/domain") and the README
/domain
/domain-wasm

# stray domain-build default outputs (built at repo root per the README/
# examples/challenges instructions, e.g. `domain build challenges/02_two_sum.domain -o two_sum`)
/day1
/day4
/day5
/day8
/day8_full
/aoc2020_day1_part2
/all_pairs_count
/bindiag
/common_letters
/dijkstra
/enumerate_dot
/fixed_point
/fold_hash
/gear_ratios
/grade_points
/life
/max_by
/modpow_sum
/part1
/regions
/s5g
/s5h
/sort_by
/two_sum
/union_count
/window_max
